<h1>Week 2: Data Acquisition and Preparation Report</h1>

<p class="center">
<b>Intern Name:</b> [Your Full Name]<br>
<b>Role:</b> Junior Machine Learning Data Analyst Intern<br>
<b>Focus Area:</b> Agriculture &amp; Agribusiness<br>
<b>Date:</b> August 2026<br>
<b>Week:</b> 2 of 4
</p>

<h2>1. Executive Summary</h2>

<p>
This report documents the data acquisition and preparation phase of the machine learning data analysis project in agriculture and agribusiness. The objective of Week 2 is to identify suitable publicly available agricultural datasets, assess their quality, and design a structured data preparation pipeline that transforms raw agricultural data into a clean and analysis-ready format.
</p>

<p>
Agricultural data often comes from multiple sources such as soil testing records, weather stations, satellite or climate APIs, farm management systems, and national agricultural statistics. These datasets commonly contain missing values, inconsistent units, outliers, duplicate records, and formatting issues. Therefore, data preparation is a critical step before applying machine learning models.
</p>

<p>
This document explains the data sourcing strategy, data selection criteria, data quality assessment, cleaning methods, normalization approach, outlier treatment, feature engineering, and the end-to-end pipeline used to prepare the dataset for modelling. It also discusses the challenges encountered during the process and the solutions applied to overcome them.
</p>

<h2>2. Objective of Week 2</h2>

<p>The main objectives of Week 2 are:</p>

<ul>
<li>To identify publicly available agricultural datasets relevant to agribusiness decision-making.</li>
<li>To evaluate the quality and suitability of the selected datasets.</li>
<li>To document data quality issues such as missing values, outliers, duplicates, and inconsistent formatting.</li>
<li>To design a step-by-step data cleaning and preprocessing pipeline.</li>
<li>To prepare the data for future machine learning analysis, including crop yield prediction and crop suitability classification.</li>
</ul>

<h2>3. Data Sourcing Strategy</h2>

<p>
The data sourcing strategy focused on collecting open, reliable, and agriculture-relevant datasets. Since the project is based on machine learning for agribusiness, the selected data needed to include variables that directly affect agricultural productivity, such as soil nutrients, weather conditions, crop type, and yield performance.
</p>

<h3>3.1 Selected Data Sources</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Data Source</th>
<th>Type of Data</th>
<th>Purpose</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Kaggle Crop Recommendation / Soil Dataset</td>
<td>Soil nutrients, pH, temperature, humidity, rainfall, crop type</td>
<td>Crop suitability classification and soil-health analysis</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>FAOSTAT Agricultural Statistics</td>
<td>Crop production, area harvested, yield per hectare</td>
<td>Yield benchmarking and regression target support</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>NASA POWER / NOAA Climate Data</td>
<td>Temperature, rainfall, humidity, solar radiation</td>
<td>Weather-based feature enrichment</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Open Soil Information Sources</td>
<td>Soil pH, nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, moisture</td>
<td>Soil quality and fertility analysis</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>3.2 Criteria for Data Selection</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Selection Criterion</th>
<th>Reason</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Relevance to agriculture</td>
<td>The data must contain features that influence crop growth, soil fertility, irrigation, or agricultural productivity.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Public availability</td>
<td>The datasets must be openly accessible for educational and analytical use.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Structured format</td>
<td>Tabular CSV data was preferred because it is suitable for supervised machine learning.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sufficient record count</td>
<td>The dataset should contain enough rows to allow meaningful exploratory analysis and model training.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Agronomic interpretability</td>
<td>The features should be understandable from an agribusiness perspective, such as rainfall, soil pH, nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2 class="page-break">4. Data Quality Assessment</h2>

<p>
Before cleaning, the raw dataset was profiled to understand its structure, completeness, consistency, and potential issues. Data profiling involved checking the number of records, column types, missing values, duplicate rows, statistical summaries, and unusual values.
</p>

<h3>4.1 Dataset Overview</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Attribute</th>
<th>Details</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Dataset type</td>
<td>Structured tabular agricultural data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Expected record count</td>
<td>Approximately 2,000 to 2,500 records</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Main features</td>
<td>Nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium, soil pH, rainfall, temperature, humidity, crop type, yield</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Target variables</td>
<td>Crop type for classification; crop yield for regression</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>File format</td>
<td>CSV</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>4.2 Identified Data Quality Issues</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Issue</th>
<th>Observed Example</th>
<th>Impact</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Missing values</td>
<td>Missing rainfall, humidity, or soil pH values</td>
<td>Can reduce model accuracy and create bias if not handled correctly</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Duplicate records</td>
<td>Repeated farm or plot entries</td>
<td>Can cause overrepresentation of certain observations</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Outliers</td>
<td>Extremely high rainfall or impossible soil pH values</td>
<td>Can distort statistical summaries and model training</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Inconsistent categories</td>
<td>“Maize”, “maize”, and “MAIZE”</td>
<td>Creates unnecessary category duplication</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Unit inconsistency</td>
<td>Temperature in Celsius and Fahrenheit</td>
<td>Can mislead distance-based algorithms and analysis</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Negative or invalid values</td>
<td>Negative yield or pH above 14</td>
<td>Indicates data entry or sensor errors</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>5. Data Cleaning Methods</h2>

<h3>5.1 Error Checking</h3>

<p>
The first cleaning step involved checking for structural and logical errors. This included verifying column names, checking data types, identifying impossible values, and ensuring that agricultural features were within realistic ranges.
</p>

<p>Examples of error checks applied:</p>

<ul>
<li>Soil pH should normally fall between 3.5 and 9.5 for agricultural soil.</li>
<li>Rainfall values should not be negative.</li>
<li>Yield values should not be negative.</li>
<li>Temperature values should be converted to a single unit.</li>
<li>Crop names should be standardized to lowercase.</li>
</ul>

<h3>5.2 Handling Missing Values</h3>

<p>
Missing values are common in agricultural data because of sensor failures, incomplete lab tests, manual entry errors, or missing weather station records. The handling method depends on the type of feature and the amount of missing data.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Feature Type</th>
<th>Missing Value Strategy</th>
<th>Reason</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Soil pH</td>
<td>Median imputation</td>
<td>Soil pH is usually stable within a region, and median imputation reduces the effect of outliers.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rainfall and humidity</td>
<td>KNN imputation or regional average imputation</td>
<td>Weather values are often similar across nearby locations and similar seasons.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Nitrogen, phosphorus, potassium</td>
<td>Median imputation</td>
<td>Median values are robust and suitable for soil nutrient measurements.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Target variable: crop type</td>
<td>Drop missing rows</td>
<td>Missing target values cannot be used for supervised classification.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Target variable: yield</td>
<td>Drop missing rows for regression task</td>
<td>Yield imputation could introduce bias and data leakage.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>5.3 Duplicate Record Treatment</h3>

<p>
Duplicate records were identified using all feature columns. If two records had identical values across all relevant fields, they were considered duplicates. Duplicate rows were removed to prevent the model from learning repeated observations more strongly than unique observations.
</p>

<h3>5.4 Outlier Treatment</h3>

<p>
Outlier treatment was performed using both statistical methods and agricultural domain knowledge. Statistical methods such as the Interquartile Range, also known as IQR, were used to detect unusually high or low values. However, final outlier decisions were based on realistic agricultural limits.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Feature</th>
<th>Outlier Rule</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Soil pH</td>
<td>Values below 3.5 or above 9.5</td>
<td>Capped or replaced with regional median</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Rainfall</td>
<td>Values above 99th percentile or physically unrealistic values</td>
<td>Capped to a realistic maximum threshold</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Temperature</td>
<td>Values outside realistic seasonal range</td>
<td>Checked and corrected based on regional climate data</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Yield</td>
<td>Negative values</td>
<td>Removed or corrected depending on context</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>5.5 Data Normalization and Scaling</h3>

<p>
Normalization and scaling were applied to ensure that numerical features are on comparable ranges. This is especially important for machine learning models that rely on distance calculations, such as K-Nearest Neighbours, Support Vector Machines, and some clustering methods.
</p>

<p>The following scaling approaches were considered:</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Method</th>
<th>Use Case</th>
<th>Reason</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>StandardScaler</td>
<td>Soil nutrients, rainfall, temperature</td>
<td>Transforms data to zero mean and unit variance; useful for many ML algorithms.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>MinMaxScaler</td>
<td>Features with known bounded ranges</td>
<td>Scales values between 0 and 1, useful for neural networks or distance-based models.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>RobustScaler</td>
<td>Data with many outliers</td>
<td>Uses median and interquartile range, reducing the effect of extreme values.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p>
For this project, StandardScaler was selected as the primary scaling method because it performs well with many common machine learning algorithms and preserves useful information about the distribution of agricultural features.
</p>

<h3>5.6 Categorical Data Transformation</h3>

<p>
Categorical variables such as crop type, soil type, season, and region needed to be converted into numerical format before modelling.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Categorical Feature</th>
<th>Encoding Method</th>
<th>Reason</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Season</td>
<td>One-hot encoding</td>
<td>Season categories do not have a natural numerical order.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Soil type</td>
<td>One-hot encoding</td>
<td>Prevents the model from assuming false ordering between soil types.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Region</td>
<td>Target encoding or grouping into agro-ecological zones</td>
<td>Regions may have many categories, so grouping improves stability.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Crop type</td>
<td>Label encoding for target variable</td>
<td>Required for classification modelling.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2 class="page-break">6. Feature Engineering</h2>

<p>
Feature engineering was used to create more meaningful agricultural variables from the raw dataset. This improves the ability of machine learning models to detect patterns related to crop growth and productivity.
</p>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Engineered Feature</th>
<th>Description</th>
<th>Agricultural Relevance</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>NPK balance ratio</td>
<td>Ratio of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium</td>
<td>Crop growth depends on nutrient balance, not only absolute nutrient levels.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Thermal stress index</td>
<td>Combination of high temperature and low humidity</td>
<td>Helps identify heat stress conditions that may reduce yield.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Water availability index</td>
<td>Combination of rainfall and soil moisture</td>
<td>Represents crop water availability during the growing season.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Seasonal rainfall total</td>
<td>Total rainfall during crop growth period</td>
<td>More useful than isolated daily rainfall measurements.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Regional average yield</td>
<td>Historical average yield by region</td>
<td>Provides context for local productivity levels.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>7. Data Preparation Pipeline</h2>

<p>
The data preparation pipeline describes the full process from raw data to structured analysis-ready data. The pipeline is designed to be repeatable, transparent, and suitable for machine learning.
</p>

<h3>7.1 Pipeline Steps</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Step</th>
<th>Activity</th>
<th>Output</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>1</td>
<td>Load raw dataset</td>
<td>Raw DataFrame</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>2</td>
<td>Inspect structure and data types</td>
<td>Data profile summary</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3</td>
<td>Standardize column names</td>
<td>Clean column names</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>4</td>
<td>Remove duplicates</td>
<td>Unique records only</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>5</td>
<td>Correct invalid agronomic values</td>
<td>Validated agricultural ranges</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>6</td>
<td>Handle missing values</td>
<td>Complete or imputed dataset</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>7</td>
<td>Treat outliers</td>
<td>Reduced distortion from extreme values</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>8</td>
<td>Engineer new features</td>
<td>Enhanced agricultural features</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>9</td>
<td>Encode categorical variables</td>
<td>Numerical model inputs</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>10</td>
<td>Scale numerical features</td>
<td>Normalized feature values</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>11</td>
<td>Split into training and testing sets</td>
<td>Model-ready dataset</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>7.2 Final Structured Dataset</h3>

<p>
After preprocessing, the final dataset is structured so that each row represents one agricultural observation, such as a farm plot or regional record, and each column represents a clean numerical or encoded feature.
</p>

<p>The final dataset is suitable for:</p>

<ul>
<li>Crop type classification.</li>
<li>Crop yield regression.</li>
<li>Soil health analysis.</li>
<li>Feature importance analysis.</li>
<li>Exploratory data visualization.</li>
</ul>

<h2>8. Challenges Encountered and Solutions</h2>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Challenge</th>
<th>Description</th>
<th>Solution</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Different data formats</td>
<td>Datasets came from different sources with different column names and units.</td>
<td>Column names were standardized and units were converted to a common format.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Missing weather data</td>
<td>Some rainfall and humidity values were missing due to sensor or recording issues.</td>
<td>KNN imputation or region-based imputation was used to preserve local patterns.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Unrealistic soil values</td>
<td>Some pH values were outside realistic agricultural ranges.</td>
<td>Values were capped or replaced based on agronomic knowledge.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Temporal mismatch</td>
<td>Weather data was daily, while yield data was annual.</td>
<td>Daily weather data was aggregated into seasonal averages and totals.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Category inconsistency</td>
<td>Crop names had different capitalization and spacing.</td>
<td>All categorical text fields were cleaned and standardized.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Class imbalance</td>
<td>Some crop categories had fewer records than others.</td>
<td>Stratified sampling was used during train-test split, and class distribution was documented.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h2>9. Replicability of the Process</h2>

<p>
To ensure that the data preparation process can be replicated, the following practices were applied:
</p>

<ul>
<li>All cleaning steps are documented in a logical sequence.</li>
<li>A Python pipeline script is included in the repository.</li>
<li>Random seeds are fixed for reproducible train-test splitting.</li>
<li>Feature engineering formulas are clearly defined.</li>
<li>Outlier thresholds are based on documented agronomic ranges.</li>
<li>The final dataset is saved after preprocessing for future modelling.</li>
</ul>

<h2>10. Conclusion</h2>

<p>
The data acquisition and preparation process completed during Week 2 provides a strong foundation for the machine learning analysis to be performed in Week 3. The project successfully identified relevant agricultural data sources, assessed data quality issues, and applied appropriate cleaning and transformation techniques.
</p>

<p>
The prepared dataset is now structured, cleaned, and ready for exploratory analysis and model development. The pipeline is designed to be reproducible and practical for agribusiness applications, where data quality and domain knowledge are essential for reliable machine learning outcomes.
</p>

